    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d263">

                <pb facs="0457" n="367" sameAs="frus:frus1932v02/pg367"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">862.4061 Motion Pictures/60</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany (Sackett) to the Acting Secretary
                            of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1653</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-04-14">April 14, 1932</date>.<lb/>[Received April
                        30.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: As of possible interest to the Department,
                    I have the honor to enclose herewith copy of a memorandum of a conversation
                    between Trade Commissioner Canty, of the
                    Commercial Attaché’s office, and Dr. Henke, the film expert of the Foreign Office, with regard to the <pb
                        facs="0458" n="368" xml:id="pg368"/> German and international film
                    situation. The interview was arranged at the request of the Embassy.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Frederic M.
                                Sackett</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d263-a1.xml" xpointer="d263-a1"/>
            </div>
